  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   Central idea: Optimize a piecewise-constant function using a multi-resolution strategy combined with combinatorial search. The key insight is that optimal solutions for similar minimum overlap problems often exhibit patterns that favor a higher degree of symmetry and periodicity. We will use a hierarchical optimization where we start with a coarse grid and refine the solution by increasing the resolution.  \n   Rationale: The current best is 0.380898, and the target is 0.38085. By exploiting the periodic structure of the problem, we can identify stable patterns that generalize well across resolutions. Unlike previous approaches that relied solely on local optimization, we incorporate global combinatorial search to escape local minima.  \n   Limitations: This approach assumes that optimal patterns are periodic and that higher grids capture more refined structures. The computational cost grows with grid resolution, and we require fallback mechanisms to handle rough regions or time constraints.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   Representation: $h \\in \\{0, 1\\}^{n_{\\text{points}}$ with $\\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2$  \n   Objective: $C_5 = \\max_{k} \\int_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx \\approx \\frac{\\max(\\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}'full')}{n_{\\text{points}}} \\times 2$  \n   Constraints: $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$ and $\\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2$  \n   Equivalence: The discrete correlation approximates the continuous integral. The factor of 2 comes from the integral normalization with $dx = 2/n_{\\text{points}}$.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   Phase 1: Initialization  \n   - Given `initial_h_values` (n=384), validate or generate the initial solution.  \n   - Pad the grid to multiples of 128: $n = 256, 512, 1024, 2048$ (capped at 2048 to balance cost).  \n   - Project initial values to satisfy $\\sum h = n/2$ using orthogonal projection onto the subspace $h_i = 0.5$ for all $i$ except the adjusted terms.  \n\n   Phase 2: Optimization  \n   - Use simulated annealing with a dynamic cooling schedule:  \n     - Track improvements over 20 steps to decide to quit early.  \n     - Use $\\delta = 0.9999$ for the temperature update.  \n   - Incorporate combinatorial search:  \n     - Flip two bits randomly at each step, ensuring $\\sum h$ remains constant.  \n     - Temporally separate the binary search with smooth interpolation for increased exploration.  \n\n   Phase 3: Multi-resolution trial  \n   - Evaluate candidates at grid sizes $[384, 512, 1024, 2048]$ in increasing order of cost.  \n   - Use prediction based on scaled grid size: $n_{\\text{trial}} = \\lceil n_{\\text{current}} \\times 1.2 \\rceil$ for incremental trials.  \n   - If any trial yields $C_5 < \\text{best}$, keep it and refine it further.  \n\n   Phase 4: Final polishing  \n   - For the best candidate, perform 200 steps of simulated annealing.  \n   - Inject randomization: flip 0.1% of bits at each step to escape local optima.  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n   - Default resolution: Start at 384, then $n \\in \\{512, 768, 1024, 2048\\}$.  \n   - Simulated annealing: $T_{\\text{start}} = 1000$, $T_{\\text{final}} = 0.01$, use logarithmic cooling schedule.  \n   - Combinatorial steps: Always attempt binary flip first before grid expansion.  \n   - Grid expansion: Only if current $C_5 > 0.385$ (probability-based heuristic).  \n   - Fallback: If time expires, return the last candidate with the smallest $C_5$.  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   - Dominant cost: $O(n^2)$ for correlation at each candidate. For n=2048, 9e8 comparisons.  \n   - Schedule:  \n     - First grid (384): 60% of total time.  \n     - Subsequent grids: Decreasing proportion to prevent overflow.  \n   - Time checks: Internal `remaining_time()` calculations every 5 function evaluations.  \n   - Failure response: Downgrade to the best-finding strategy at grid level 384.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   Feasibility: $\\sum h = n/2$ and $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$. Final checks ensure sum equals $n/2$.  \n   Objective: Re-evaluate $C_5$ for all recovered candidates.  \n   Final candidate must pass: $C_5 < \\min_{\\text{parent}} C_5 - 1e-7$.  \n   Return $(h, C_5, n)$ formatted for evaluation.",
